  <div class="sl">Likutay Halachos &middot; Yoreh De&apos;ah, Part Two</div>
  <div class="mt">Laws of Chalah</div>
  <div class="st">Hilchos Chalah</div>
  <div class="dv">&middot; &middot; &#10022; &middot; &middot;</div>
  <div class="ht">Halacha Four</div>
  <div class="pt">Part Two &mdash; Sections &#x05D6;&ndash;&#x05D9;</div>

  <div class="sm first">Section Seven &middot; &#x05D6;</div>

  <p class="ni">And therefore one who makes his entire dough into <em>Chalah</em> has done nothing, as it is said: <em>&ldquo;raishis&rdquo;</em> [firstfruits] &mdash; meaning that its remainder (<em>shirayim</em>) must be recognizable &mdash; as is explained in the <em>Shulchan Aruch</em>. For this is the essential rectification: that through the <em>raishis</em> [first portion] which one separates, the <em>shirayim</em> [remainder] too are rectified and ascend to holiness, in the aspect of <em>&ldquo;v&rsquo;nish&rsquo;ar gam hu le&rsquo;Elokaynu&rdquo;</em> [and he shall remain also to our G-d]. <em>&ldquo;V&rsquo;nish&rsquo;ar&rdquo;</em> [and he shall remain] specifically &mdash; meaning that the aspect of the <em>shirayim</em>, the aspect of <em>&ldquo;v&rsquo;nish&rsquo;ar gam hu le&rsquo;Elokaynu,&rdquo;</em> it too shall ascend to Hashem blessed-be-He, to its supernal root. And this is the essential wholeness of Creation &mdash; which was created for this: that the <em>shirayim</em> too should ascend to holiness through the <em>raishis</em>, which is the essential thing for whose sake everything was created, as it is written: <em>&ldquo;Beraishis bara Elokim&rdquo;</em> [In the beginning G-d created] etc. (Beraishis 1:1). And our Sages of blessed memory expounded: <em>&ldquo;for the sake of Israel who are called the firstfruits of His produce, and for the sake of the Torah which is called the beginning of His way&rdquo;</em> etc. (Rashi, Beraishis 1:1). It turns out that Israel and the Torah are the essential <em>raishis</em> for whose sake everything was created &mdash; the heavens and the earth and all that is in them. And therefore certainly the heavens and the earth etc. &mdash; all of them are called <em>shirayim</em> in relation to the Torah and Israel who are the <em>raishis</em>.</p>

  <p>And the essential rectification is when one knows who is the <em>raishis</em> and who is the <em>shirayim</em>. And all the world and its fullness which are the <em>shirayim</em> &mdash; when they toil on their behalf (<em>torchim bisvilam</em>), then the <em>shirayim</em> too are rectified through the <em>raishis</em>, and then everything returns to Hashem blessed-be-He in the aspect of <em>&ldquo;v&rsquo;nish&rsquo;ar gam hu le&rsquo;Elokaynu&rdquo;</em> &mdash; as will be in the future. But as long as one does not separate the <em>raishis</em> and does not know who is the <em>raishis</em> and who is the <em>shirayim</em> &mdash; and all the more so when, heaven forbid, one inverts the truth and makes the tail into a head etc. &mdash; then the whole world is disordered, heaven forbid. And this is the aspect of <em>&ldquo;and the earth was&rdquo;</em> etc. (Beraishis 1:2) &mdash; for at the beginning of Creation, which was for the sake of the <em>raishis</em> so that through it the <em>shirayim</em> might be rectified as mentioned &mdash; but then, at the start, who was the <em>raishis</em> had not yet been revealed and was not recognizable. For the holiness of Israel and the Torah, which are the <em>raishis</em>, had not yet been revealed. And therefore it was then said: <em>&ldquo;and the earth was tohu vavohu [formless and void] and darkness&rdquo;</em> etc. (ibid.) &mdash; for as long as the <em>raishis</em> is not revealed, the whole world is disordered in the aspect of <em>tohu vavohu</em> and darkness etc. &mdash; as now in the exile, when Israel who are the <em>raishis</em> are subdued in exile and they [the nations] are at the head etc.</p>

  <p>And this is what the Midrash expounded: <em>tohu vavohu</em> and darkness refer to the exiles of the four kingdoms &mdash; as they said there: <em>&ldquo;tohu &mdash; this is the kingdom of Babylon&rdquo;</em> etc., <em>&ldquo;vavohu&rdquo;</em> etc. That is: <em>tohu vavohu</em> etc. alludes to the time of the exiles when Israel are subdued in exile and the <em>raishis</em> &mdash; for whose sake everything was created &mdash; is not recognizable. But the essential sustenance in the exile is through the aspect of <em>&ldquo;and the spirit of G-d&rdquo;</em> &mdash; <em>da rucho shel Mashiach</em> [this is the spirit of Mashiach] &mdash; <em>&ldquo;hovered over the face of the waters&rdquo;</em> &mdash; <em>da Orayta</em> [this is the Torah] (Zohar, Beraishis 2a) &mdash; for Mashiach is destined to redeem us speedily, and he also sustains and preserves us throughout all the days of the exile through the Torah that illuminates in us and informs us that Hashem is still with us, and that we should not despair of Him blessed-be-He. For we still have hope &mdash; every single one of us, in general and in particular. And through this: <em>&ldquo;and G-d said: let there be light, and there was light&rdquo;</em> (Beraishis 1:3) &mdash; for through this Hashem blessed-be-He illuminates for us at every moment, until we merit to discern who is the <em>raishis</em> and who is the <em>shirayim</em>.</p>

  <p>And this is the aspect of <em>&ldquo;and G-d separated between the light and the darkness&rdquo;</em> (Beraishis 1:4) &mdash; which are the aspects of <em>raishis</em> and <em>shirayim</em>. For the light is in the East, which is called <em>kedem</em> [before/east], the aspect of <em>raishis</em>; and the darkness, which is the setting of the light, is in the West, which is called <em>achor v&rsquo;sof</em> [behind and end], the aspect of <em>shirayim</em>. And when there is separation between the light and the darkness, between the <em>raishis</em> and the <em>shirayim</em> &mdash; then is the essential rectification of the world, that the <em>shirayim</em> too are rectified through the <em>raishis</em>, in the aspect of <em>&ldquo;v&rsquo;nish&rsquo;ar gam hu le&rsquo;Elokaynu&rdquo;</em> as mentioned. And this is: <em>&ldquo;and there was evening and there was morning, day one&rdquo;</em> (Beraishis 1:5) &mdash; that the evening and the morning are included together, which are the <em>raishis</em> and the <em>shirayim</em>, and both are included in the unity of the world in the aspect of <em>&ldquo;nish&rsquo;ar gam hu&rdquo;</em> etc. &mdash; which is the aspect of <em>&ldquo;day one&rdquo;</em> (<em>yom echad</em>), said because Hashem blessed-be-He was alone (<em>yachid</em>) in His world (Rashi, ad loc.). For specifically through Hashem blessed-be-He separating between the light and the darkness &mdash; that is, between the <em>raishis</em> and the <em>shirayim</em> &mdash; and one knows who is the <em>raishis</em> and who is the <em>shirayim</em>: then specifically the <em>shirayim</em> too are rectified and included in Hashem blessed-be-He, in the aspect of <em>&ldquo;v&rsquo;nish&rsquo;ar gam hu le&rsquo;Elokaynu,&rdquo;</em> the aspect of <em>&ldquo;and there was evening and there was morning, day one&rdquo;</em> as mentioned.</p>

  <div class="sm">Section Eight &middot; &#x05D7;</div>

  <p class="ni">And this is the secret of <em>Havdalah</em> (<em>sod haHavdalah</em>) &mdash; that specifically through making <em>Havdalah</em> and saying <em>&ldquo;HaMavdil bain kodesh l&rsquo;chol&rdquo;</em> [Who separates between the holy and the mundane], specifically through this the weekdays too are sanctified, and the holiness of Shabbos is drawn upon them, and the grip of the serpent&rsquo;s defilement (<em>zuhamas hanachash</em>) is expelled from the weekdays &mdash; as is known, for this is the essential secret of <em>Havdalah</em>. And on the face of it this seems the opposite &mdash; for are we not making separation between the holiness of Shabbos and the weekdays? So how are the weekdays sanctified through this? But it is as mentioned: for specifically through the <em>Havdalah</em> by which one separates between the holy and the mundane, between the seventh day and the six days of labor, and one knows and reveals that the essential holiness and the essential sustenance of the world is Shabbos, and all the weekdays are the aspect of <em>shirayim</em> which were all created only for the sake of Shabbos &mdash; specifically through this the weekdays too are sanctified with the holiness of Shabbos. Through the fact that they toil throughout all the weekdays for the sake of Shabbos &mdash; this being their essential elevation and rectification &mdash; as it is written: <em>&ldquo;Remember the day of Shabbos&rdquo;</em> &mdash; remember it from the first day of the week (Mechilta, Shemos 20:8) etc.</p>

  <p>And through this the six weekdays are included in the holiness of Shabbos and are sanctified from the serpent&rsquo;s defilement. For as long as one does not make separation between the holiness of Shabbos and the weekdays, and does not reveal the difference, it appears heaven forbid as though all faces are equal and one does not know who is the <em>raishis</em> and who is the <em>shirayim</em> &mdash; then the world is in the aspect of <em>tohu vavohu</em> and darkness etc. But specifically through <em>Havdalah</em>, which reveals the greatness of the holiness of Shabbos as the essential thing and the <em>raishis</em> &mdash; the aspect of <em>&ldquo;the first among the holy convocations&rdquo;</em> (<em>techillah l&rsquo;mikra&rsquo;ei kodesh</em>) (Vayikra 23:2 &mdash; as the Sages expounded of Shabbos) &mdash; specifically through this the <em>shirayim</em>, which are the weekdays, are also sanctified. For this is the essential rectification of the world and its fullness, which was created for this, for the sake of the <em>raishis</em> as mentioned &mdash; that is, that the whole world was created for the sake of Israel and the Torah, so that all would toil and labor for the sake of Israel and the Torah who are the essential thing and the <em>raishis</em>.</p>

  <p>And likewise among Israel themselves: the masses and the <em>amay ha&rsquo;aretz</em> [common people] must toil and labor to support the <em>talmiday chachamim</em> [Torah scholars] and the G-d-fearing who occupy themselves with His service blessed-be-He, for they are the essential <em>raishis</em>. And likewise throughout all the weekdays one must toil for the sake of the holiness of Shabbos as mentioned. And then specifically, when one knows who is the <em>raishis</em> and who is the <em>shirayim</em>, and the <em>shirayim</em> toil for the sake of the <em>raishis</em> &mdash; then the <em>shirayim</em> too are sanctified and included in the <em>raishis</em>, in the aspect of <em>&ldquo;v&rsquo;nish&rsquo;ar gam hu le&rsquo;Elokaynu&rdquo;</em> as mentioned. And therefore in the commandment of <em>Chalah</em> there must be specifically <em>raishis</em> and <em>shirayim</em>, as it is written: <em>&ldquo;raishis,&rdquo;</em> that its remainder (<em>shirayim</em>) be recognizable &mdash; for this is the essential wholeness and rectification, that the <em>shirayim</em> be rectified through the <em>raishis</em> as mentioned.</p>

  <p>And this is what our Sages of blessed memory expounded the verse <em>Beraishis</em> upon <em>Bikkurim</em>, <em>Terumah</em>, and <em>Chalah</em> &mdash; which are called <em>raishis</em> &mdash; that for their sake the world was created. For it is all one: for all the commandments of <em>Chalah</em>, <em>Terumah</em>, and <em>Bikkurim</em> are all in the aforementioned aspect &mdash; to sanctify and to separate the <em>raishis</em>, so that through this the <em>shirayim</em> too are clarified and rectified. And this itself is the whole of Creation: that the whole world and its fullness &mdash; which is the aspect of <em>shirayim</em> &mdash; was created for the sake of the <em>raishis</em>, which is Israel and the Torah as mentioned. For it is all one as mentioned.</p>

  <div class="sm">Section Nine &middot; &#x05D8;</div>

  <p class="ni">And this is: <em>&ldquo;If there is no flour, there is no Torah&rdquo;</em> (<em>im ein kemach ein Torah</em>) (Avos 3:17). The words of Torah are compared to a millstone (<em>rechayim</em>), as our Sages of blessed memory said: just as the millstone never ceases etc. (Midrash). For the clarification (<em>berur</em>) of the flour from which bread is made is like the clarification of Torah &mdash; which requires precise examination (<em>dikduk</em>) and thorough grinding until the <em>halachah</em> is clarified in its truth. And then one merits the <em>nahama d&rsquo;Orayta</em> [bread of Torah], the aspect of <em>&ldquo;come, eat of my bread&rdquo;</em> (<em>l&rsquo;chu lachamu b&rsquo;lachmi</em>) (Mishlay 9:5).</p>

  <p>And this itself is the clarification of the flour: one must grind the wheat into flour so that the sparks are divided very, very finely &mdash; in the aspect of the <em>ketores</em> [incense] of which it is said <em>&ldquo;and you shall grind some of it very fine&rdquo;</em> (<em>v&rsquo;shachakta mimena hadek</em>) (Shemos 30:36), and when one grinds it one says: <em>&ldquo;grind it fine, fine, grind it&rdquo;</em> (<em>hadek haytiv haytiv hadek</em>). And on Yom Kippur, when one must clarify and raise from the deepest depths of the <em>klippos</em> more and more &mdash; for then all sins are atoned &mdash; therefore then the <em>ketores</em> must be <em>dakah min hadakah</em> [fine beyond fine]. For this is the general rule: the more that sparks require rectification and clarification, the more they need to be divided into ever more parts &mdash; as is explained in <em>Sefer HaGilgulim</em> [Book of Transmigrations].</p>

  <p>And this is the aspect of flour: it is impossible to make bread unless one first grinds the wheat into flour, which is ground and divided into very, very fine particles &mdash; so that the sparks are divided into very many parts, so that we can then specifically gather them and clarify them and make from them dough and bread. Then they are included in the stature of man (<em>komat adam</em>) through the man eating them &mdash; and they are the essential vitality and satisfaction of man: namely the sparks which are the divine vitality (<em>chiyus Elokyus</em>) within them, from which comes the essential satisfaction and life-force. And this is what Rashi explained regarding the creation of the first Adam: <em>&ldquo;he kneaded the dough&rdquo;</em> (<em>lash es ha&rsquo;isah</em>) (Sanhedrin 38b) &mdash; for it is all one. For the dust of the first Adam was gathered from all parts of the world, for all the sparks and all the souls were included in him &mdash; as is known. And this is the aspect of dough (<em>isah</em>) made from flour as mentioned &mdash; which is the aspect of Torah, the aspect of <em>&ldquo;if there is no flour, there is no Torah.&rdquo;</em></p>

  <p>And this is why the clarification of the <em>halachos</em> by <em>talmiday chachamim</em> was compared to the clarification of foods &mdash; as the Mishnah says: <em>&ldquo;there are four types among students: a sponge, a funnel, a strainer, and a sieve&rdquo;</em> (<em>sefog, mashpech, mishemeres, nafah</em>) (Avos 5:15).</p>

  <p>And therefore the essential completion of the clarification is through the <em>Chalah</em> separated from the dough made of flour and water &mdash; the waters of <em>da&rsquo;as</em> [knowledge] and Torah drawn upon the flour. And through this one gathers and collects it and kneads it and makes dough. And then it is obligated in <em>Chalah</em>: to give the <em>raishis</em> to the Kohen who is composed of <em>ben v&rsquo;talmid</em>, through which the entire dough is included in the aforementioned aspect, in the aspect of Torah and <em>da&rsquo;as</em>, through which all the sparks ascend etc. as mentioned. And therefore <em>Chalah</em> is practiced even now outside the Land of Israel, because through <em>Chalah</em> comes the essential clarification as mentioned.</p>

  <div class="sm">Section Ten &middot; &#x05D9;</div>

  <p class="ni">And this is why the Torah portion of <em>Chalah</em> is juxtaposed with the Torah portion of the Spies (<em>Meraglim</em>) in the portion <em>Shelach Lecha</em> (Bamidbar 13&ndash;15). For the Spies blemished the Land of Israel and blemished the aspect of the illumination of <em>ben v&rsquo;talmid</em> &mdash; as is understood from the aforementioned Torah. For they passed through the Land of Israel when it was still in exile under the hand of the seven nations. And when they came there they were seized with fears, and they were placed there in a state of smallness and lowliness (<em>katnus v&rsquo;shiplut</em>), in the aspect of exile, as it is written: <em>&ldquo;and we were in our own eyes as grasshoppers&rdquo;</em> (<em>vanhi v&rsquo;ainaynu kachagavim</em>) etc. (Bamidbar 13:33).</p>

  <p>But they ought to have strengthened themselves to believe in Hashem and in Moshe His servant, that through his great power He would conquer everything before them, and everything would be returned to Hashem blessed-be-He and to Israel &mdash; as indeed happened afterward in truth. But they denied this. And therefore it was decreed upon them weeping for generations, that Israel must linger so long in exile. And through the fact that there are even now tzaddikim who strengthen us to believe in Hashem blessed-be-He that He will conquer everything before us, and everything will ascend to Hashem blessed-be-He in the aspect of <em>&ldquo;v&rsquo;nish&rsquo;ar gam hu le&rsquo;Elokaynu&rdquo;</em> &mdash; through this we rectify the sin of the Spies who blemished this. And through this Mashiach Tzidkaynu will come and redeem us speedily, and everything will return to Hashem blessed-be-He as mentioned. And this is the aspect of <em>Chalah</em> to the Kohen, which is the aspect of <em>&ldquo;v&rsquo;nish&rsquo;ar gam hu le&rsquo;Elokaynu.&rdquo;</em> And therefore the portion of <em>Chalah</em> is juxtaposed in the portion of <em>Shelach Lecha</em> as mentioned.</p>

  <p>And this is also the aspect of the portion of <em>Minchas Nesachim</em> [flour-offerings and libations] (Bamidbar 15:1&ndash;16) which is juxtaposed immediately with the portion of the Spies, and then right afterward the portion of <em>Chalah</em>. For <em>menachos</em> [flour-offerings] are brought from flour, where the essential clarification resides &mdash; which is the aspect of <em>Chalah</em> as mentioned. And therefore the essential drawing close of Israel to Hashem blessed-be-He at the Exodus from Egypt and the Receiving of the Torah was through the flour-offering of the <em>Omer</em> on Pesach and the <em>Shtay HaLechem</em> [Two Loaves] on Shavuos. For <em>menachos</em> and <em>Chalah</em> which come from flour &mdash; which are the rectification of bread, which is the essential food &mdash; there is the illumination of the Will which is the essential rectification, drawn through the illumination of <em>ben v&rsquo;talmid</em>. Which is the essential <em>da&rsquo;as</em> [knowledge] to know Hashem, which is drawn on Pesach and Shavuos. And therefore it is juxtaposed with the portion of the Spies who blemished all of this. For through <em>menachos</em> and <em>Chalah</em>, which are the rectification of bread, through which one merits the illumination of the Will etc. &mdash; through this the blemish of the Spies is rectified. For through this we will merit to go out from exile, and all the sparks will be clarified and ascend, and speedily it will be fulfilled: <em>&ldquo;v&rsquo;nish&rsquo;ar gam hu le&rsquo;Elokaynu&rdquo;</em> etc. as mentioned.</p>

  <div class="clos">&#10022; &nbsp; <em>Bimhayrah b&rsquo;yamaynu, Amain.</em> &nbsp; &#10022;</div>
